.TH lsb.resources 5 "Volclava Version 2.2 - August 2026"
.br
.SH NAME
\fBlsb.resources\fR
.SS \fB\fROverview
.BR
.PP
.PP
The lsb.resources file contains configuration information for resource
allocation limits. It is stored in the directory LSF_ENVDIR, where
LSF_ENVDIR is defined in the lsf.conf file. This file is optional. 
.PP
Each limit restricts how much of a resource (such as slots,
memory, or a custom resource) can be consumed simultaneously by jobs
that match a given set of hosts, queues, users, and projects. If the
file is not present, no resource allocation limits are enforced.
.PP
This manual describes the Volclava resource limit format, which is the
default and recommended format. The file is also compatible
with the LSF limit definition format; see
.SB "LSF Compatibility"
at the end of this manual.
.SS Changing lsb.resources configuration
.BR
.PP
.PP
After changing the lsb.resources file, run \fBbadmin reconfig or badmin mbdrestart\fR to
reconfigure mbatchd.
.PP
During reconfiguration or restart, mbatchd re-reads lsb.resources and rebuilds the
in-memory resource limit configuration. The new limits take effect on the
next scheduling pass after reconfiguration completes; running jobs are
not affected by the new configuration.
.SH Limit Section
.BR
.PP
.PP
The Limit section sets limits for the maximum amount of the specified
resources that must be available for different classes of jobs to start,
and which hosts, queues, users, or projects the limits apply to.
Limits are enforced during job resource allocation.
.PP
The \fBblimits\fR command displays current usage of resource allocation
limits configured in Limit sections in lsb.resources.
.SH Limit Section Structure
.BR
.PP
.PP
The file contains one or more limit definitions. Each limit begins
with the line \fBBegin Limit\fR and ends with the line \fBEnd Limit\fR.
Section names are matched case-insensitively; any section other than
\fBLimit\fR is ignored.
.PP
Inside a limit block, parameters are specified as \fBKEY = value\fR or
\fBKEY=value\fR pairs, one per line. Whitespace around \fB=\fR is
optional, and lines beginning with \fB#\fR are treated as comments.
.PP
A minimal limit must define at least \fBNAME\fR and one resource entry
in \fBRESOURCES\fR. The definitions recognized in a Limit section are:
.RS
.HP 2
\(bu \fBNAME\fR - name of the limit (required).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBHOSTS\fR - hosts and host groups the limit applies to (optional).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBQUEUES\fR - queues the limit applies to (optional).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBUSERS\fR - users and user groups the limit applies to (optional).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBPROJECTS\fR - projects the limit applies to (optional).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBRESOURCES\fR - the resource(s) and their limit values (required).
.HP 2
\(bu \fBDESCRIPTION\fR - free-form description of the limit (optional).
.RE
.PP
The \fBHOSTS\fR, \fBQUEUES\fR, \fBUSERS\fR, and \fBPROJECTS\fR fields
each wrap their name list in \fBPER(...)\fR or \fBSHARED(...)\fR:
\fBPER(...)\fR enforces the limit separately for each matching host,
queue, user, or project, while \fBSHARED(...)\fR enforces a single
shared limit across all the listed entries. The four fields may use
different modes; for example \fBHOSTS=PER(all)\fR together with
\fBUSERS=SHARED(ug1)\fR is valid. The following sections describe
each definition's syntax and usage.
.SH NAME
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBNAME = \fR\fIlimit_name\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The name of the limit. Required.
.PP
Specify any ASCII string 60 characters or less. You can use letters, digits,
underscores (_) or dashes (-). You cannot use blank spaces. The limit is skipped
if \fBNAME\fR is missing, empty, invalid, or a duplicate.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined
.SH HOSTS
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBHOSTS = \fR\fBPER(\fR\fIhost ...\fR\fB)\fR | \fBHOSTS = \fR\fBSHARED(\fR\fIhost ...\fR\fB)\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The hosts and/or host groups to which the limit applies. Optional.
.PP
The value must be wrapped in either \fBPER(...)\fR or
\fBSHARED(...)\fR. \fBPER(...)\fR keeps a separate usage counter for
each matching host; \fBHOSTS=PER(hostA hostB)\fR with
\fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows 4 slots on \fBhostA\fR and a
separate 4 slots on \fBhostB\fR. \fBSHARED(...)\fR maintains a single
shared counter across all the listed hosts; \fBHOSTS=SHARED(hostA
hostB)\fR with \fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows a combined total of
4 slots across \fBhostA\fR and \fBhostB\fR.
.PP
\fIhost\fR can be a host name, a host group name (resolved against
\fBlsb.hosts\fR), or one of the special tokens:
.RS
.HP 2
\(bu \fBall\fR - matches every host in the cluster; name validation
is skipped.
.HP 2
\(bu \fB~name\fR - excludes \fIname\fR from the match. Exclusion
takes priority over positive matches.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBgroup\fR or \fBgroup/\fR - a host group name. The trailing
slash is optional; when present, \fIgroup\fR is matched only as a host
group (for example \fBhg1\fR or \fBhg1/\fR).
.RE
.PP
Unknown host or host group names are reported as warnings and removed
from the list; the limit itself is still accepted. The keywords
\fBall\fR and \fBdefault\fR skip name validation.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined (the limit is not restricted by the host, which is equal to the value SHARED(all)).
.SH QUEUES
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBQUEUES = \fR\fBPER(\fR\fIqueue ...\fR\fB)\fR | \fBQUEUES = \fR\fBSHARED(\fR\fIqueue ...\fR\fB)\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The queues to which the limit applies. Optional. The value must be
wrapped in \fBPER(...)\fR or \fBSHARED(...)\fR. \fBPER(...)\fR
enforces the limit separately for each matching queue;
\fBQUEUES=PER(q1 q2)\fR with \fBRESOURCES=[JOBS, 10]\fR allows 10
jobs in \fBq1\fR and a separate 10 jobs in \fBq2\fR.
\fBSHARED(...)\fR enforces a single shared limit across all the listed
queues; \fBQUEUES=SHARED(q1 q2)\fR with \fBRESOURCES=[JOBS, 10]\fR
allows a combined total of 10 jobs across \fBq1\fR and \fBq2\fR.
.PP
\fIqueue\fR is a queue name defined in \fBlsb.queues\fR, or the
special token \fBall\fR (matches every queue). The \fB~\fR exclusion
prefix is also supported. Unknown queue names are warned about and
removed; the limit is still accepted.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined (the limit is not restricted by the queue, which is equal to the value SHARED(all)).
.SH USERS
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBUSERS = \fR\fBPER(\fR\fIuser ...\fR\fB)\fR | \fBUSERS = \fR\fBSHARED(\fR\fIuser ...\fR\fB)\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The users and/or user groups to which the limit applies. Optional.
.PP
The value must be wrapped in either \fBPER(...)\fR or
\fBSHARED(...)\fR. \fBPER(...)\fR keeps a separate usage counter for
each matching user; \fBUSERS=PER(userA userB)\fR with
\fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows 4 slots for \fBuserA\fR and a
separate 4 slots for \fBuserB\fR. \fBSHARED(...)\fR maintains a single
shared counter across all the listed users; \fBUSERS=SHARED(userA
userB)\fR with \fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows a combined total of
4 slots across jobs submitted by \fBuserA\fR and \fBuserB\fR.
.PP
\fIuser\fR can be a user name, a user group name (resolved against
\fBlsb.users\fR), or one of the special tokens:
.RS
.HP 2
\(bu \fBall\fR - matches every user in the cluster; name validation
is skipped.
.HP 2
\(bu \fB~name\fR - excludes \fIname\fR from the match. Exclusion
takes priority over positive matches.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBgroup\fR or \fBgroup/\fR - a user group name. The trailing
slash is optional; when present, \fIgroup\fR is matched only as a user
group (for example \fBug1\fR or \fBug1/\fR).
.RE
.PP
Unknown user or user group names are reported as warnings and removed
from the list; the limit itself is still accepted. The keywords
\fBall\fR and \fBdefault\fR skip name validation.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined (the limit is not restricted by the user, which is equal to the value SHARED(all)).
.SH PROJECTS
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBPROJECTS = \fR\fBPER(\fR\fIproject ...\fR\fB)\fR | \fBPROJECTS = \fR\fBSHARED(\fR\fIproject ...\fR\fB)\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The projects to which the limit applies. Optional. The value must be
wrapped in \fBPER(...)\fR or \fBSHARED(...)\fR. \fBPER(...)\fR
enforces the limit separately for each matching project;
\fBPROJECTS=PER(p1 p2)\fR with \fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows 4
slots for \fBp1\fR and a separate 4 slots for \fBp2\fR.
\fBSHARED(...)\fR enforces a single shared limit across all the listed
projects; \fBPROJECTS=SHARED(p1 p2)\fR with
\fBRESOURCES=[SLOTS, 4]\fR allows a combined total of 4 slots across
\fBp1\fR and \fBp2\fR.
.PP
\fIproject\fR is a project name, or the special token \fBall\fR
(matches every project). The \fB~\fR exclusion prefix is also
supported. Project names are not currently validated against a
project table; any non-empty token is accepted.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined (the limit is not restricted by the project, which is equal to the value SHARED(all)).
.SH RESOURCES
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBRESOURCES = \fR\fB[\fR\fIresource_name\fR\fB,\fR \fIvalue\fR\fB]\fR [\fIresource_name\fR\fB,\fR \fIvalue\fR\fB%\fR] ...
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
The resource(s) the limit enforces, together with their limit values.
At least one resource entry is required; a limit without any resource
is skipped.
.PP
Each entry is written as \fB[\fR\fIresource_name\fR\fB,\fR \fIvalue\fR\fB]\fR.
Multiple entries are concatenated, optionally separated by whitespace;
for example:
.PP
\fBRESOURCES = [SLOTS, 10] [MEM, 50%] [lic1, 5]\fR
.PP
\fIvalue\fR：A non-negative number. 
.PP
\fIresource_name\fR： If it matches one of the
built-in resource names listed below, the corresponding built-in
semantics apply. Otherwise it must be a resource already defined in
\fBlsf.shared\fR; an unknown custom resource name causes the limit to
be skipped.
.PP
The singular keyword \fBRESOURCE\fR is not accepted in the Volclava
format. Always use the plural \fBRESOURCES\fR.
.SH Built-in Resource Names
.BR
.PP
.PP
The following resource names have built-in meaning:
.RS
.HP 2
\(bu \fBJOBS\fR - the number of jobs that may run concurrently under
the limit. Each running job counts as 1 toward this value, regardless
of how many slots or processors it uses.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBSLOTS\fR - the total number of job slots that may be in use.
Each running job contributes \fInumProcessors\fR toward this value.
Mutually exclusive with \fBSLOTS_PER_PROCESSOR\fR.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBMEM\fR - memory, in units determined by
\fBLSF_UNIT_FOR_LIMITS\fR in \fBlsf.conf\fR (default MB). The value is
a non-negative number or a percentage (suffix \fB%\fR), computed
against the host's \fBmaxMem\fR capacity. When a percentage is used,
\fBHOSTS\fR must be \fBPER(...)\fR.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBSWP\fR - swap, in \fBLSF_UNIT_FOR_LIMITS\fR units. The value
is a non-negative number or a percentage (suffix \fB%\fR), computed
against the host's \fBmaxSwap\fR. When a percentage is used,
\fBHOSTS\fR must be \fBPER(...)\fR.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBTMP\fR - temporary file space, in \fBLSF_UNIT_FOR_LIMITS\fR
units. The value is a non-negative number or a percentage (suffix
\fB%\fR), computed against the host's \fBmaxTmp\fR. When a percentage
is used, \fBHOSTS\fR must be \fBPER(...)\fR.
.HP 2
\(bu \fBSLOTS_PER_PROCESSOR\fR - the number of job slots allowed per
processor on the host. The value is a non-negative number, and
\fBHOSTS\fR must be \fBPER(...)\fR. The host capacity used for this
computation is \fBnumCPUs\fR. Mutually exclusive with \fBSLOTS\fR.
.RE
.PP
Any other name is treated as a custom (shared or host-based) resource
taken from \fBlsf.shared\fR, for example \fBlic1\fR or \fBgpu\fR.
.PP
A limit that omits \fBHOSTS\fR or uses \fBHOSTS = SHARED(...)\fR
together with a percentage \fBMEM\fR, \fBSWP\fR, or \fBTMP\fR, or
with \fBSLOTS_PER_PROCESSOR\fR, is rejected and skipped.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR
.PP
.SS Syntax
.BR
.PP
.PP
\fBDESCRIPTION = \fR\fI"text"\fR
.SS Description
.BR
.PP
.PP
Free-form description of the limit. Optional. The text is truncated to
4095 characters if it is longer.
.SS Default
.BR
.PP
.PP
Undefined
.SH LSF Compatibility
.BR
.PP
.PP
In addition to the Volclava format described above, the file is also
compatible with the traditional LSF limit definition in horizontal format;
the format is auto-detected per limit block and the two cannot be mixed
within a single block.
.PP
New configurations should prefer the Volclava format, which is the
default and recommended format.
.SH Examples
.BR
.PP
.PP
A cluster-wide limit of 100 slots:
.PP
.nf
\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = cluster_slots
\&RESOURCES = [SLOTS, 100]
\&End Limit
.fi
.PP
A per-queue limit of 50 jobs on the \fBnormal\fR queue:
.PP
.nf
\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = normal_jobs
\&QUEUES = PER(normal)
\&RESOURCES = [JOBS, 50]
\&End Limit
.fi
.PP
A per-host limit: 4 slots and 50% of memory on each of \fBhostA\fR
and \fBhostB\fR:
.PP
.nf
\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = per_host_mem
\&HOSTS = PER(hostA hostB)
\&RESOURCES = [SLOTS, 4] [MEM, 50%]
\&End Limit
.fi
.PP
A shared limit: \fBuserA\fR and \fBuserB\fR together may use 8
licenses; \fBuserA\fR is excluded from a separate per-user slots
limit of 10:
.PP
.nf
\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = shared_lic
\&USERS = SHARED(userA userB)
\&RESOURCES = [lic, 8]
\&End Limit

\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = per_user_slots
\&USERS = PER(all ~userA)
\&RESOURCES = [SLOTS, 10]
\&End Limit
.fi
.PP
A fully specified limit using all four fields (HOSTS, QUEUES, USERS,
PROJECTS):
.PP
.nf
\&Begin Limit
\&NAME = full_limit
\&HOSTS    = PER(hostA hostB)
\&QUEUES   = PER(normal short)
\&USERS    = SHARED(ug1 ~user1)
\&PROJECTS = PER(default proj1)
\&RESOURCES = [JOBS, 3] [SLOTS, 10]
\&DESCRIPTION = "Demo full limit"
\&End Limit
.fi
